.TH CBMAP_INSERT_ELEM 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cbmap_insert_elem \- insert a key-value pair into the BST map
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

ccol_retval_t cbmap_insert_elem(cbmap cbm,
                                 const cmap_pair *key_pair,
                                 const cmap_pair *val_pair);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Inserts a key-value pair into the BST map. If the key is already in the
map, the function replaces the old value. It then balances the tree
again with AVL rotations.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cbm        BST map to insert into
key_pair   Key to insert (the function copies it into the map)
val_pair   Value for that key (the function copies it into the map)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success               - The function inserted a new key
ccol_key_already_present   - The key was already in the map, and the
                             function updated its value
ccol_container_full        - The map holds ccol_max_elem_count elements
ccol_not_enough_memory     - A memory allocation failed
ccol_invalid_args          - key_pair or val_pair is NULL, one of the
                             two pairs carries a NULL ptr with a
                             non-zero size, or the size of key_pair
                             does not match the size of a fixed-width
                             key type (see cbmap_create_full(3))
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The ptr of a key_pair needs no particular alignment. The comparison
reads the key bytes with memcpy and never through a pointer cast to the
key type. A caller can therefore point key_pair->ptr straight at key
bytes that sit at an arbitrary offset inside a larger buffer, such as a
field sliced out of a packed network frame or a memory-mapped record,
without copying them into an aligned local first.
O(log n) complexity
The function copies the key data and the value data into the map
If the key is already in the map, the function frees the old value and
puts the new value in its place
The function asserts if cbm is NULL
The function reports a NULL key_pair or a NULL val_pair as
ccol_invalid_args. This matches chmap_insert_elem(3)
A pair whose ptr is NULL and whose size is not zero promises bytes that
it has no storage for. The function reports it as ccol_invalid_args too
A size of 0 is correct for the key or the value, with a ptr or without
one. It stores an empty key or an empty value. The BST map differs from
chmap_insert_elem(3) here, because that function rejects a key or a
value of size zero
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_insert (3),
.BR cbmap_delete_elem (3),
.BR cbmap_get_elem_ref (3)
